  <h2>Keep it to one page</h2>
  <p>As a student or recent graduate, your CV should be a single side of A4. Architects reviewing applications are busy and design-literate - they will judge how you organise a page as much as what it says. A tight one-pager forces you to prioritise, and it signals editorial discipline. Save the depth for your portfolio; the CV is the trailer, not the film. If you are struggling to fit it, you are almost certainly including things that do not earn their space.</p>

  <h2>Layout and typography</h2>
  <p>This is architecture - your CV is a small design exercise and people will notice. Use a clear hierarchy, generous margins and one or two typefaces at most. Align things properly, keep consistent spacing, and let white space do some work. Avoid clip-art icons, rainbow colour and skill "percentage bars", which read as filler. A restrained, well-set document looks more professional than a busy one. Export as a PDF so it opens looking exactly as you designed it, and name the file sensibly, for example Firstname-Lastname-CV.pdf.</p>

  <h2>What to include</h2>
  <p>Cover the essentials cleanly and cut the rest.</p>
  <table class="data"><tr><th>Section</th><th>What goes here</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Contact</td><td>Name, email, phone, city, portfolio link</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Education</td><td>Degree, institution, dates, notable results or awards</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Experience</td><td>Placements and jobs, with what you actually did</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Software</td><td>CAD, Revit, Rhino, Adobe suite, model-making</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Skills &amp; extras</td><td>Languages, hand drawing, relevant volunteering</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Under experience, describe what you contributed rather than just your job title - "produced planning drawings and physical models for a housing scheme" says far more than "intern". Even non-architecture jobs count if you frame the transferable skills: reliability, working with clients, hitting deadlines.</p>

  <h2>Link your portfolio</h2>
  <p>Your portfolio is the main event, so make the link impossible to miss - near the top, clearly labelled, and working. Use a clean URL to an online portfolio or a hosted PDF, and test it on a phone. Many students also attach a short sample of a few standout pages alongside the CV. Whatever you do, never make an architect hunt for your work. See our <a href="/career/architecture-internship/">internship guide</a> for how to pair the two in an application.</p>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Tip.</strong> Ask someone to read your CV for fifteen seconds, then take it away and tell you what they remember. If your strongest project or skill is not what they recall, your layout is burying the wrong things.</div>

  <h2>Tailor it</h2>
  <p>A generic CV is a weak CV. Before you send it, look at the practice's work and adjust accordingly - reorder your software list to lead with the tools they use, foreground the experience closest to their sector, and make sure your portfolio link leads with relevant projects. This takes a few minutes per application and dramatically raises your hit rate. Practices can tell instantly whether you have engaged with who they are or fired off a template.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <p>A handful of errors sink otherwise strong applications. Typos and inconsistent formatting are the worst, because in a design field they read as carelessness - proofread, then have a friend proofread again. Do not spill onto a second page with padding. Do not use decorative fonts that hurt legibility. Do not send a giant file that will not open, or a portfolio link that is broken or private. And do not write one CV and blast it everywhere unchanged. Fixing these puts you ahead of a large share of the pile before anyone has judged your design work at all.</p>

  <h2>A final check</h2>
  <p>Before you hit send, run through a quick list: one page, PDF, working portfolio link, tailored to this practice, no typos, sensible filename, and a named recipient in the email. It is a small ritual that consistently separates the applications that get read from the ones that get skimmed and closed.</p>

  <div class="callout warn"><strong>Routes vary.</strong> Qualification paths, titles and figures differ by country and change over time - always check the current requirements of your national registration body (e.g. ARB/RIBA in the UK, NCARB in the US).</div>

